### Create one child bead per sub-task

For every approved sub-task, create a child bead depending on the epic (so
`Loop processing tasks` can pick them up later if the operator ever enables it):

```bash
bd create "<sub-task title>" \
  --type task \
  --priority 1 \
  --label on-call,incident,investigation \
  --parent <EPIC_ID> \
  --body-file /tmp/subtask-<n>.md
```

Record every returned bead ID; call them `<SUB_IDS>`.

### Dispatch one curious investigator per sub-task

For each `<SUB_ID>`, spawn a child conversation
`mitto_conversation_new(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}", beads_issue: "<SUB_ID>", title: "Investigate <sub-task title>", initial_prompt: <see below>)`.
Reuse **idle** existing children (`{{ Default "(none)" .Children.MCPText }}`) if
any match; otherwise create fresh. Cap at **4 parallel** investigators — if the
approved list has more sub-tasks than that, queue the overflow by asking
`mitto_ui_options` whether to dispatch the top-4 now and queue the rest, or
reduce the list.

The initial prompt for each investigator MUST:

- State the incident title, the parent `<EPIC_ID>`, and the child's own
  `<SUB_ID>`, and remind the child to keep its bead updated (`bd update`,
  `bd comment`) as it works. Bead comments should follow markdown conventions.
- Instruct the child to attach itself to its bead first:
  `mitto_conversation_update(self_id: <own id>, conversation_id: "self", beads_issue: "<SUB_ID>")`.
- Give the child the consolidated situation summary so it does not re-ingest.
- **Inline the investigation playbook** you loaded above verbatim (between `---`
  fences) so the child follows the same runbook — do NOT paraphrase or summarise
  it. If the playbook cites a specific section relevant to this sub-task, call it
  out: *"This sub-task maps to §<section> of the playbook — start there."*
- Set the child's mindset explicitly: **"You are a curious, inquisitive
  investigator. Ask 'why?' at every layer, follow evidence rather than
  assumptions, distinguish observation from hypothesis, and report both
  dead-ends and leads."**
- Tell the child, at the end of the initial prompt, to report via
  `mitto_children_tasks_report(self_id: <own id>, task_id: "investigate", status: "completed" | "partial" | "failed", summary: "<findings>", details: "<evidence, commands run, links, open questions>")`
  **and** to write its full findings back to its `<SUB_ID>` bead as comments
  before reporting.

### Wait, synthesize, and hand back to the operator

Block on the investigators:

```
mitto_children_tasks_wait(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}", children_list: [<SUB conversation ids>], task_id: "investigate", timeout_seconds: 3600)
```

Then:

1. Read every sub-bead's fresh comments (`bd comments <SUB_ID>`) alongside the
   child reports.
2. Merge findings by hypothesis, flag conflicts / gaps / open questions, and rank
   the surviving hypotheses.
3. Append a **"Round-1 synthesis"** comment to `<EPIC_ID>` with the ranked
   hypotheses, the strongest supporting evidence, and the next investigation moves.
4. Notify the operator:
   `mitto_ui_notify(self_id: "{{ .Session.ID }}", title: "Round-1 triage complete", message: "<EPIC_ID>: <N> sub-tasks investigated — see the epic for the synthesis.", style: "success", beads_issue: "<EPIC_ID>")`.
5. Ask via `mitto_ui_options` whether to (a) dispatch a follow-up round based on
   the surviving hypotheses, (b) hand back to the operator, or (c) stop.

If the operator picks (a), loop back to the decomposition step (a new
decomposition based on the surviving hypotheses — the playbook is still in your
context, no need to reload it); otherwise stop — do NOT propose or execute
mitigation. Mitigation is a human decision.
